      <h2 id="best-allinone-clickup">#1 — ClickUp (Best All-in-One Notion Alternative)</h2>
      <p>ClickUp is the most direct all-in-one Notion alternative because it combines tasks, docs, goals, and databases in a single platform — the same value proposition as Notion, but with stronger task management. ClickUp Docs support rich text, embeds, and table views, while tasks, lists, and boards handle project tracking. For teams that find Notion's task management too lightweight, ClickUp is the natural upgrade.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is interface complexity. ClickUp has more features than any team needs on day one, and onboarding takes longer than Notion. The free tier is generous but limited in storage and automation runs.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>ClickUp</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>All-in-one platform</strong> — Tasks, docs, goals, dashboards, and whiteboards</li>
          <li><strong>ClickUp Docs</strong> — Rich text docs with embeds, tables, and task linking</li>
          <li><strong>15+ views</strong> — List, board, Gantt, calendar, timeline, workload</li>
          <li><strong>ClickUp AI</strong> — AI writing, summaries, and task autofill</li>
          <li><strong>Free tier</strong> — Unlimited tasks, 100MB storage, 60+ integrations</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://clickup.com" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Try ClickUp Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-local-obsidian">#2 — Obsidian (Best for Local-First Knowledge Graph)</h2>
      <p>Obsidian is the best Notion alternative for individuals who want local-first, offline-first note-taking with a powerful knowledge graph. Notes are stored as plain Markdown files on your local drive — no cloud lock-in, no subscription required, and full ownership of your data. The bidirectional backlinks and graph view create a visual map of how your notes connect.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is collaboration. Obsidian is primarily a single-user tool; real-time team editing requires the paid Sync and Publish services. For personal knowledge management, nothing beats Obsidian's speed and data portability. See our <a href="/reviews/notion-vs-obsidian/">Notion vs Obsidian comparison</a> for the full breakdown.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Obsidian</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Local-first Markdown</strong> — Files live on your device, fully offline</li>
          <li><strong>Bidirectional backlinks</strong> — Link notes and visualize connections in graph view</li>
          <li><strong>4,000+ community plugins</strong> — Extend with calendars, kanban, dataview, and more</li>
          <li><strong>Free for personal use</strong> — Unlimited notes, no subscription, no account required</li>
          <li><strong>Sync &amp; Publish</strong> — Optional paid services for cross-device sync and web publishing</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://obsidian.md" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Download Obsidian Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-interactive-coda">#3 — Coda (Best for Interactive Docs & Formulas)</h2>
      <p>Coda is the best Notion alternative for teams that need interactive documents with real formulas, buttons, and automations. Coda's formula language is Turing-complete, meaning you can build lightweight internal tools, calculators, and workflow apps inside a document. For teams that outgrow Notion's formula limitations, Coda is the next step up in interactivity.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is pricing and adoption. Coda's Pro plan at $12/user/month is more expensive than Notion Plus, and the formula learning curve is steeper. Coda is best for teams that specifically need doc-as-an-app functionality.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Coda</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Powerful formula language</strong> — Turing-complete formulas with row references and conditional logic</li>
          <li><strong>Interactive buttons</strong> — Trigger automations, update rows, and send notifications from doc</li>
          <li><strong>Packs</strong> — 600+ integrations including Slack, Jira, Gmail, and custom API packs</li>
          <li><strong>Doc-as-an-app</strong> — Build lightweight internal tools without code</li>
          <li><strong>Free tier</strong> — Unlimited docs with limited rows and objects</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://coda.io" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Try Coda Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-capture-evernote">#4 — Evernote (Best for Capture & Web Clipping)</h2>
      <p>Evernote remains the best tool for capturing and organizing web content. The web clipper extension is the most polished in the category — it clips full pages, articles, screenshots, and bookmarks with one click, then makes them searchable alongside your typed notes. For researchers, journalists, and anyone who collects web research, Evernote's capture workflow is unmatched.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is pricing and the block-based editor. Evernote's free tier now limits to 50 notes per month, and the Personal plan at $14.99/month is more expensive than Notion Plus. The editor is note-centric, not block-based, so it lacks Notion's flexible page building. See <a href="/reviews/notion-vs-evernote/">Notion vs Evernote</a> for the full comparison.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Evernote</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Web Clipper</strong> — Best-in-class browser extension for clipping pages, articles, and screenshots</li>
          <li><strong>Document scanning</strong> — Built-in OCR for scanning receipts, business cards, and documents</li>
          <li><strong>Search</strong> — Full-text search across typed notes, images, and PDFs</li>
          <li><strong>Templates</strong> — 50+ templates for meeting notes, journals, and project tracking</li>
          <li><strong>Integrations</strong> — Google Drive, Slack, Outlook, and Teams</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://evernote.com" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Try Evernote Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-microsoft-onenote">#5 — Microsoft OneNote (Best for Microsoft 365 Teams)</h2>
      <p>OneNote is the best Notion alternative for organizations already standardized on Microsoft 365. It is included in every M365 plan at no additional cost, integrates natively with Teams, SharePoint, and Outlook, and supports enterprise security policies including Information Protection and DLP. For large organizations, OneNote is often the path of least resistance.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is that OneNote is a digital notebook, not a structured workspace. It lacks Notion's database views, block-based page building, and template ecosystem. Teams that need structured data management will still need Notion or another tool alongside OneNote.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Microsoft OneNote</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Included in M365</strong> — No additional cost for organizations with Microsoft 365</li>
          <li><strong>Notebook structure</strong> — Notebooks, sections, and pages for hierarchical organization</li>
          <li><strong>Inking &amp; drawing</strong> — Best-in-class stylus support for Surface and iPad</li>
          <li><strong>Teams integration</strong> — OneNote tabs in Teams channels for shared meeting notes</li>
          <li><strong>Enterprise security</strong> — Information Protection, DLP, and sensitivity labels</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/onenote/digital-note-taking-app" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Get OneNote Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="selection-scenarios">Selection Scenarios</h2>

      <div class="use-case">
        <h3>Individual Power User / Researcher</h3>
        <p><strong>Recommendation: Obsidian (free) or Evernote (for web research).</strong> Obsidian gives you a local-first knowledge graph with full data ownership. Evernote is better if your primary workflow is clipping web articles and making them searchable. Both are stronger than Notion for individual capture and PKM workflows.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="use-case">
        <h3>Team That Outgrew Notion's Task Management</h3>
        <p><strong>Recommendation: ClickUp.</strong> If your team uses Notion for docs but finds its project management too lightweight, ClickUp is the most direct all-in-one upgrade. Migrate docs into ClickUp Docs and use tasks, boards, and Gantt views for project tracking.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="use-case">
        <h3>Enterprise on Microsoft 365</h3>
        <p><strong>Recommendation: Microsoft OneNote (included).</strong> If your organization already pays for M365, OneNote is free, secure, and integrates with Teams. Reserve Notion for teams that specifically need structured databases and block-based page building.</p>
      </div>

