      <h2 id="buying-guide">Buying Guide: How to Choose a Notion Alternative in 2026</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Step 1 — Identify your Notion pain point:</strong> Is it pricing, data ownership, formula limitations, capture, or Microsoft integration? Each alternative excels at a different gap.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 2 — Test the free tier:</strong> Obsidian, ClickUp, Coda, and OneNote all have free tiers. Import a real Notion project and test for 2 weeks before paying.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 3 — Verify data portability:</strong> Can you export your data as Markdown or another open format? Obsidian and OneNote score highest on data portability.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 4 — Check collaboration needs:</strong> Single-user tools (Obsidian) vs team tools (ClickUp, Coda, OneNote) — pick based on how many people will edit simultaneously.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 5 — Calculate total cost:</strong> OneNote is free with M365. Obsidian is free for personal use. ClickUp and Coda charge per user. Model 1-year and 3-year costs.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Learn more about our <a href="/how-we-test/">testing methodology</a> and <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the best free alternative to Notion in 2026?</summary>
          <p>Obsidian is the best free alternative to Notion for individuals who want local-first, offline note-taking. Obsidian's free tier includes unlimited notes, backlinks, graph view, and 4,000+ community plugins. The trade-off is that Obsidian stores files locally as Markdown, so real-time collaboration requires a paid sync service. For team collaboration, ClickUp's free tier is the closest Notion alternative with tasks, docs, and databases.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is Obsidian better than Notion for knowledge management?</summary>
          <p>Obsidian is better than Notion for personal knowledge management (PKM) because its bidirectional backlinks, graph view, and local Markdown files create a durable, portable knowledge graph that you own. Notion is better for team wikis and structured databases because it is cloud-native and collaborative. Many power users run both: Obsidian for personal notes and Notion for shared team docs.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can Coda replace Notion for team documentation?</summary>
          <p>Coda can replace Notion for teams that need interactive documents with real formulas, buttons, and automations. Coda's formula language is more powerful than Notion's, and its doc-as-an-app model lets you build lightweight internal tools. However, Notion has a larger template ecosystem, better search, and broader adoption. Coda is best for teams that outgrow Notion's formula limitations.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is Microsoft OneNote a good Notion alternative for enterprise?</summary>
          <p>Microsoft OneNote is the best Notion alternative for organizations already standardized on Microsoft 365. OneNote is included in every M365 plan at no additional cost, integrates natively with Teams, SharePoint, and Outlook, and supports enterprise security policies. The trade-off is that OneNote lacks Notion's database views, block-based page building, and template ecosystem — it is a digital notebook, not a structured workspace.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

